{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# S3 — Bound journald storage and alarm on journal saturation\n\n## Why\n\nADR 0112 decisions 1+2, the **journald** accumulator. At the 2026-09-02 outage the root\nvolume's 28G working set included `/var/log` at 1.8G, of which the **journal was 1.7G** — every\ncompose service logs to the host journal, so journald is a genuine root-volume generator. The\nonly signal was `rebar-root-disk-pressure`: \"root disk high\", which cannot name which of the\nfour accumulators grew, and naming it cost `du` under time pressure.\n\n## What actually bounds journald, and how strongly — stated before the design\n\n`SystemMaxUse=` is a **real cap enforced by the writer itself**, not a sweeper and not\nretention-by-convention: journald checks it **synchronously as it extends a journal file** and\nvacuums to stay under it. That makes it materially STRONGER than S2's image/layer share (which\nhas no ceiling at all and is held only by retention). Two residuals, both written into the\ntree rather than glossed:\n\n1. **Only ARCHIVED files are deleted.** The ACTIVE journal file is never vacuumed, so usage may\n   exceed `SystemMaxUse` by up to one file. That overshoot is bounded by `SystemMaxFileSize`,\n   which defaults to **1/8 of `SystemMaxUse`** — so the true worst case is `SystemMaxUse` ×\n   9/8, i.e. **3.375 GiB for the 3 GiB cap below**, not 3 GiB exactly.\n2. **It bounds `/var/log/journal`, not `/var/log`.** Anything else under `/var/log` is outside\n   this cap. At the incident that remainder was ~0.1G of the 1.8G, so it is small — but it is\n   unbounded by this story and covered only by the root-disk backstop.\n\n## The trap this plan must not walk into: journald is ALREADY capped\n\n`SystemMaxUse=` is not unset-means-unbounded. Its default is **10% of the filesystem, capped at\n4G** — on this 60 GiB root that resolves to **4 GiB**, and the journal measured 1.7G, i.e. it\nwas *inside* its implicit ceiling at the outage. A plan that claimed to \"bound an unbounded\njournal\" would be false. What this story actually buys, precisely:\n\n- **An explicit, smaller ceiling** — 3 GiB rather than the derived 4 GiB — that is **pinned\n  regardless of volume size**, so a future root resize cannot silently move it.\n- **A ceiling that is READABLE.** The derived default exists only inside journald's head; no\n  file on the box states it, so no operator can confirm it during an incident.\n- **Naming the generator.** Journal growth today is invisible until it shows up inside\n  \"root disk high\" — which is the exact gap ADR 0112 decision 2 exists to close.\n\n## Design\n\n### 1. `infra/scripts/journald-cap.sh` (new) — the single source of truth\n\nSame shape as S2's `docker-storage-cap.sh`, deliberately, so the two caps read alike:\n\n- `--print-env` — `JOURNAL_MAX_USE_BYTES`, `JOURNAL_DIR`, `JOURNALD_DROPIN`. Side-effect-free;\n  consumed by `observability.sh` with `eval` so **the published \"percent of cap\" and the cap\n  journald is told to enforce are the same number by construction** and cannot drift.\n- `--print-conf` — renders the drop-in to stdout, writes nothing (the `--print-volumes` /\n  `--print-json` precedent), so rendering is testable with no root and no systemd.\n- `--check-active` — prints `1`/`0`, the observation described below.\n- `--install` — render, no-op fast path, write, restart journald, then OBSERVE.\n\nMeasured, env-settable default (ADR 0112 decision 6): **`JOURNAL_MAX_USE_BYTES=3221225472`\n(3 GiB)** — 1.76× the 1.7G measured at the incident, 25% below the derived 4 GiB it replaces,\nand independent of the volume size.\n\nThe drop-in is `/etc/systemd/journald.conf.d/99-rebar-disk-ceiling.conf`. The `99-` prefix is\nload-bearing, not decoration: systemd merges drop-ins in **lexicographic filename order and the\nlast assignment wins**, so the prefix makes \"ours wins\" a property rather than an accident.\n\n**Two knobs are DECLINED, with reasons, rather than set because the ticket named them:**\n\n- **`SystemKeepFree=`** — its default is 15% of the filesystem capped at 4G, i.e. **4 GiB\n  here, already at the cap**, so a resize cannot weaken it and pinning it changes nothing.\n  Pinning a *smaller* value would actively WEAKEN the free-space floor. Left derived.\n- **`RuntimeMaxUse=`** — `/run/log/journal` is tmpfs, i.e. **RAM, not the root volume**. It is\n  outside a disk-ceiling story's budget and inside the 8 GiB memory budget ADR 0079/0104 own.\n\nNo timestamped backup set is created. The drop-in is a file this script owns end to end, so\nthe operator's undo is `rm` + restart — which is also why S2's unbounded-`.bak` advisory cannot\nrecur here by construction, rather than by remembering to prune.\n\n### 2. Activation is OBSERVED, never inferred from an exit status (the S2 lesson)\n\n`journald.conf` is read by journald **at startup**. `systemd-journald.service` implements **no\n`ExecReload`**, so `systemctl reload systemd-journald` is not a way to apply this — and a\ncommand whose zero exit means nothing is exactly the false evidence S2 removed when it deleted\n`systemctl reload docker`.\n\nSo `--install` does two separate things and conflates neither:\n\n- It **restarts** `systemd-journald` when — and only when — the rendered drop-in differs from\n  what is on disk. This is safe in a way `systemctl restart docker` was not, and the difference\n  is why S2 refused a restart and this story takes one: journald's sockets are socket-activated\n  by PID 1 and its stdout stream fds are held in the service's file-descriptor store and\n  restored across the restart, so **no service goes down and no service loses its log stream**.\n  Restarting Docker, by contrast, takes Gerrit, the review-bot and the MCP server with it.\n- It then **observes** the result independently: the mtime of `/proc/<journald MainPID>` (set\n  by the kernel at process creation) against the mtime of the drop-in. `started >= written`\n  means in force. Anything undeterminable **fails closed** and is reported NOT in effect. The\n  restart's exit status is never read as activation.\n\n### 3. `observability.sh` §2g — three metrics, and both sides span the same bytes\n\nThe S2 differencing lesson generalises to a ratio: **the numerator and the denominator must\ncover the same byte set.** Here the denominator is `SystemMaxUse`, which journald applies to\nthe journal files under `/var/log/journal` — so the numerator is a bounded\n`du -sx --block-size=1` of exactly `/var/log/journal`, nothing wider and nothing narrower.\nResidual, documented: `du` counts every byte in that tree while journald's quota counts only\nits own `*.journal*` files, so stray files there **over**-report — the safe direction, since it\npages early rather than reading healthy.\n\n- `journal_bytes` (Bytes) — published only on a successful measurement.\n- `journal_used_percent` (Percent, clamped to 100 like §2f) — only when both the measurement\n  and a positive cap are in hand.\n- `journal_cap_in_effect` (Count, 1/0) — a **HEARTBEAT on every tick**, including the 0 path,\n  following `gate_scratch_mounted` (bug bff5): absence means the probe/timer/host is dead, not\n  that the cap is fine.\n\nEvery reading is gated on its own measurement succeeding: a probe that could not measure\npublishes NOTHING, and `treat_missing_data = \"breaching\"` pages on the silence. A fabricated 0\nwould read as an empty journal on a box that is filling.\n\n**`journal_cap_in_effect` closes a named gap, it is not a spare metric.** S2's own AC review\nrecorded that its BuildKit share \"stays UNCAPPED on this box until an operator schedules a\nrestart… the runbook documents the step; nothing tracks that it happened.\" Here something\ntracks it: the box asserts continuously that the cap it is measured against is the cap the\nrunning daemon actually read.\n\n### 4. Two alarms (`monitoring_autodeploy.tf`), house 300/3/2 idiom\n\n- `rebar-journal-usage-high` — `journal_used_percent` > 85, `Maximum`.\n- `rebar-journal-cap-not-in-effect` — `journal_cap_in_effect` < 1, `Minimum`\n  (the `rebar-gate-scratch-unmounted` shape).\n\nNeither implies the other, which is the 3e92 argument: a journal comfortably under a cap that\nis not in force is one restart away from unbounded, and reads perfectly healthy on the first\nalarm. Both dimensionless on both sides (the `root_disk_used_percent` convention), both\n`treat_missing_data = \"breaching\"`, both wired to `aws_sns_topic.alerts` on `alarm_actions` AND\n`ok_actions`, and both **pinned by resource label** in\n`tests/unit/test_alarm_actions_terraform.py`'s watched set with the anti-vacuity floors raised\n24 -> 26 and 19 -> 21. Terraform is WRITTEN and VALIDATED only; **`terraform apply` is an\noperator step and is not run**.\n\n### 5. Install point in `compose-up.sh`\n\nOne `if ! bash …/journald-cap.sh --install; then echo WARN; fi` block beside the existing\n`docker-storage-cap.sh` one, **NON-FATAL** for the same reason: a box without a journal ceiling\nis a capacity problem the new alarms announce, not a reason to refuse to boot the stack.\n\n## Scope\n\nIn scope: the new `journald-cap.sh`, its `compose-up.sh` install step, `observability.sh` §2g,\ntwo Terraform alarms, the runbook section, and tests. Out of scope: the compose logging driver,\nreview scratch placement (aa40), the Docker budget (9183), `/var/tmp` (S4), container layers\n(S5), and anything under `/var/log` that is not the journal.\n\n## Grounding\n\n- `infra/scripts/docker-storage-cap.sh` — the S2 cap-script shape this mirrors: `--print-env`\n  as the single source of truth, side-effect-free render modes, observed-not-inferred\n  activation via `/proc/<MainPID>` mtime, fail-closed reporting.\n- `infra/scripts/observability.sh:210-412` (§2f) — the measurement idiom: bounded probes,\n  publish-nothing-on-failure, clamped percents, dimensionless publication.\n- `infra/scripts/observability.sh:164-209` (§2e) — the 1/0 heartbeat idiom (`gate_scratch_mounted`).\n- `infra/terraform/monitoring_autodeploy.tf:324` — `rebar-gate-scratch-unmounted`, the\n  `Minimum`/`LessThanThreshold` heartbeat alarm shape.\n- `journald.conf(5)` — \"The first pair defaults to 10% and the second to 15% of the size of the\n  respective file system, but each of the calculated default values is capped to 4G\"; \"size\n  limits are enforced synchronously when journal files are extended\"; \"only archived files are\n  deleted… there might still be more space used than SystemMaxUse=… after a vacuuming\n  operation is complete\".\n- `systemd-journald.service(8)` — no `ExecReload`; configuration changes are applied by\n  restarting the service.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] AC1 — `journald-cap.sh --print-conf` renders a drop-in that sets an explicit\n      `SystemMaxUse=` from `JOURNAL_MAX_USE_BYTES`, writes nothing, and needs no root, no\n      systemd and no journald to run.\n- [ ] AC2 — `--install` is idempotent: a second run against an identical drop-in writes\n      nothing and does NOT restart journald.\n- [ ] AC3 — `--install` restarts `systemd-journald` when and only when the drop-in changed,\n      and NEVER reports \"in effect\" on the strength of that command's exit status. A test\n      pins that no `systemctl reload` is used as evidence.\n- [ ] AC4 — activation is decided by comparing the live journald start time against the\n      drop-in's mtime, and FAILS CLOSED: an unreadable PID, an unreadable mtime, or a daemon\n      predating the file is reported NOT in effect.\n- [ ] AC5 — `observability.sh` publishes `journal_bytes` and `journal_used_percent` only on a\n      successful measurement, and publishes `journal_cap_in_effect` on EVERY tick including\n      the 0 path; a failed `du` publishes no bytes and no percent rather than 0.\n- [ ] AC6 — `journal_used_percent` is computed against the SAME byte set the cap governs\n      (`/var/log/journal` vs `SystemMaxUse`) and is clamped to 100.\n- [ ] AC7 — Terraform declares `rebar-journal-usage-high` and\n      `rebar-journal-cap-not-in-effect`, both `treat_missing_data = \"breaching\"`, both wired\n      to `aws_sns_topic.alerts` on `alarm_actions` and `ok_actions`, and both pinned by\n      resource label in `tests/unit/test_alarm_actions_terraform.py` with the floors raised.\n      `terraform fmt -check` and `validate` pass; **nothing is applied**.\n- [ ] AC8 — the runbook documents the cap and its honest strength (including the\n      archived-only overshoot and the `/var/log` remainder), both alarms, diagnosis\n      (`journalctl --disk-usage`, `du -sx /var/log/journal`, `--check-active`), and safe\n      remediation (`journalctl --vacuum-size=`), and states that a cap can be installed but\n      not in force.\n- [ ] AC9 — every new test runs offline: real shell scripts over PATH stubs and a fake\n      `/proc`; no systemd, no journald, no AWS, no CI provider (the portability criterion)."}},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788560999326423942,"uuid":"cf0151c4-9414-4fd0-8436-c4e75b1888ac"}